 ## Operating characteristics
 
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N_{iters} \times N_{rand} \times N_{samples} \times C_{MLP})$, where $N_{iters}$ is the number of iterations, $N_{rand}$ is the batch size (rays per step), $N_{samples}$ is the number of samples per ray, and $C_{MLP}$ is the cost of the neural network forward pass [S5, S14, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N_{rand} \times N_{samples} \times C_{MLP})$ for the forward pass, plus $O(N_{iters})$ if storing the full computational graph for reverse-mode AD. Chunking reduces peak memory to $O(\text{chunk\_size} \times N_{samples} \times C_{MLP})$ [S5, S7, unofficial].
-*   **Measured Performance:** Training on 128x128 images for 250 epochs [S4, unofficial]. On a dataset of 199 images down-sampled by factor 4, each epoch processes ~8M rays. A batch size of 1024 results in ~7,800 iterations per epoch [S17, unofficial].
-*   **Scaling:** Larger batch sizes (e.g., $2^{14}$) hinder data loading performance and prevent full GPU occupancy, while smaller batch sizes (e.g., $2^{10}$) cap iterations per second. $2^{10}$ is often chosen as a tradeoff [S15, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Stochastic optimization allows training on large datasets by sampling subsets of rays per step, reducing compute per iteration compared to full-image rendering [S5, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Rendering quality is limited by coarse voxel grid resolution and lack of advanced ray sampling/rendering improvements found in literature, resulting in blurry predicted images [S17, unofficial]. VRAM overflow is a risk without chunking [S5, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** When optimizing a neural radiance field for novel view synthesis from multi-view images.
-*   **When not to use:** When deterministic, high-fidelity evaluation is required without stochastic perturbations (use evaluation mode instead) [S2, S4, S9, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N_{iters} \cdot N_{rand} \cdot N_{samples} \cdot C_{model})$, where $N_{iters}$ is total iterations, $N_{rand}$ is batch size (rays per step), $N_{samples}$ is samples per ray, and $C_{model}$ is the cost of a single MLP query [S5, S14, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N_{rand} \cdot N_{samples} \cdot C_{model})$ per batch, plus $O(N_{iters})$ for optimizer states (Adam maintains first and second moments) [S5, 44-radiance-field-optimization.md, unofficial]. Chunking reduces peak memory usage [S5, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance:** Training on 128x128 images typically takes 250 epochs or ~100,000 iterations [S4, S11, unofficial]. On a standard GPU, batch sizes of 1024-2048 rays are common [S5, S11, unofficial].
+*   **Scaling:** Performance scales with batch size up to VRAM limits; larger batches improve throughput but require more memory [S15, unofficial]. Smaller batch sizes (e.g., $2^{10}$) are often chosen as a trade-off between data loading performance and GPU occupancy [S15, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Simple to implement; leverages standard deep learning frameworks; produces high-quality novel views for dense input sets.
+*   **Limitations:** Slow convergence for large scenes; high memory usage for high-resolution training; prone to artifacts (floaters) in sparse-view settings; requires pre-computed camera poses (unless optimized jointly).
+*   **When to Use:** Dense-view reconstruction (e.g., Blender, LLFF datasets); high-quality offline rendering.
+*   **When Not to Use:** Real-time applications (use acceleration methods); sparse-view scenarios (use regularization/initialization methods); dynamic scenes (use time-dependent variants).
 
 ## Background
 
-The NeRF Training Loop originates from the seminal "NeRF: Representing Scenes as Neural Radiance Fields for View Synthesis" paper by Mildenhall et al. [S5, unofficial]. It was developed to solve the problem of synthesizing novel views from a sparse set of 2D images by optimizing a continuous 5D function (3D position + 2D viewing direction) represented by a neural network. The method relies on the differentiability of the volume rendering equation, allowing gradients to flow from the pixel loss back to the network weights [44-radiance-field-optimization.md]. Early implementations used TensorFlow [S5, unofficial], while later versions adopted PyTorch [S6, S13, unofficial] and JAX [S15, unofficial][S24] for better performance and ease of use. The training procedure has become a standard baseline for subsequent NeRF variants and 3D deep learning methods.
+The NeRF Training Loop was introduced by Mildenhall et al. in "NeRF: Representing Scenes as Neural Radiance Fields for View Synthesis" (2020) [S5, unofficial][S24]. It established the standard protocol of optimizing a coordinate-based MLP using stochastic gradient descent with Adam, minimizing MSE loss between rendered and ground-truth pixels. Early implementations used TensorFlow [S5, unofficial], later ported to PyTorch [S6, unofficial] and JAX [S15, unofficial][S24]. The method has become the foundational baseline for numerous extensions, including coarse-to-fine sampling [S5, unofficial], positional encoding [S20], and various regularization techniques [S7, unofficial][S8].
 
